# Concept rows: name, plain explanation, kitchen example, practical decision.
ROWS=[
("01 · Food foundations","Basic","A healthy pattern comes from varied foods across days, not a single superfood.",[
("Food pattern","What you eat repeatedly matters most","Dal, vegetables, fruit and grains across the week","Review the whole week"),("Energy","Food supplies energy for living and activity","Portion of rice or roti changes meal energy","Watch portions, not fear foods"),("Nutrients","Protein, fats, carbohydrates, vitamins and minerals do different jobs","Dal + vegetables + grain contributes several","Seek variety"),("Context","Age, activity, illness and preferences change needs","Three family members can eat different portions","Avoid one universal diet")],"Compare a plain rice-only plate with rice, dal, cooked vegetables and fruit across the day.","What is the best single food?","There is no universal best food; a varied, adequate eating pattern matters more.","Keep a three-day food pattern note, without labeling foods good or bad.",["WHO healthy diet","ICMR-NIN Indian guidelines"],["Many foods","Repeated meals","Pattern","Health"]),
("02 · Build a balanced plate","Basic","A plate model is an easy visual guide, not a rigid prescription.",[
("Vegetables and fruit","Add color and variety","Bhindi, spinach, carrot and fruit","Increase variety"),("Protein foods","Include beans, dal, eggs, fish, dairy or alternatives","Sambar plus curd or tofu","Check the protein source"),("Quality carbohydrate","Choose a suitable portion of grains or starchy foods","Rice, roti, millet or potato","Watch portion and context"),("Fats and drink","Use appropriate oils; choose water often","Small cooking-oil amount and water","Check added sugar")],"Turn idli and chutney into a fuller meal by adding sambar and vegetables; portions depend on appetite and health.","Must every meal be exactly half vegetables?","No. The visual is a starting point; overall adequacy, culture and medical needs matter.","Use the interactive plate builder and compare a familiar meal.",["WHO healthy diet","ADA diabetes plate","ICMR-NIN Indian guidelines"],["Vegetables","Protein","Grain","Whole meal"]),
("03 · Carbs, protein and fats","Basic","All three macronutrients have roles; quality and amounts matter.",[
("Carbohydrate","Main energy source, including starch and sugars","Rice and fruit both contain carbohydrate","Consider quality and portion"),("Protein","Helps maintain tissues and muscles","Dal, eggs, yogurt, tofu or fish","Include an adequate source"),("Fat","Provides energy and helps absorb some vitamins","Nuts and cooking oil","Favor unsaturated sources"),("Energy estimate","Carb/protein ≈4 kcal/g; fat ≈9 kcal/g","Recipe macro arithmetic","Estimate, do not diagnose")],"Compare equal-sized dishes: one with only refined starch and another with legumes and vegetables.","Are carbohydrates bad?","No. Type, portion and the whole meal matter.","Inspect one normal meal for all three macronutrients.",["WHO healthy diet","AHA dietary guidance"],["Carbs","Protein","Fat","Meal"]),
("04 · Protein and muscle","Intermediate","Protein needs vary; food intake and strength activity both matter for muscle.",[
("Sources","Animal and plant proteins can contribute","Dal, paneer, tofu, eggs, fish","Plan suitable variety"),("Distribution","Avoid a day with no meaningful protein until dinner","Breakfast curd, lunch dal","Notice gaps"),("Total intake","A target depends on weight, age and illness","Kidney disease can change the plan","Avoid universal targets"),("Food first","Supplements are sometimes useful but not automatic","Milk or soy before a powder decision","Check necessity")],"A vegetarian meal of dal, curd and vegetables has a clearer protein plan than plain rice alone.","Does every person need protein powder?","No. Assess diet, health and goals first; kidney disease warrants individualized advice.","List protein-containing foods in two ordinary meals.",["ICMR-NIN Indian guidelines","WHO healthy diet","NIH supplement basics"],["Food source","Meal distribution","Adequacy","Function"]),
("05 · Carbs, fiber and glucose","Intermediate","Carb portions and fiber-rich food choices affect meals, but responses differ by person.",[
("Starch","Rice, roti and potatoes contribute carbohydrate","A larger rice serving changes load","Keep portion visible"),("Fiber","Legumes, vegetables, fruit and whole grains supply fiber","Chana and vegetable curry","Increase gradually"),("Plate method","Vegetables, protein and quality carbohydrate share a meal","Rice + dal + non-starchy veg","Balance the meal"),("Personal response","Glucose can vary by quantity, activity and treatment","Same idli meal, different person","Use clinical plan if diabetic")],"Compare two idlis alone with idli, sambar and vegetable sides; do not assume one exact glucose effect.","Does brown rice erase the need to watch portion?","No. Portions and the rest of the meal still matter.","Build a meal with legumes and vegetables alongside starch.",["ADA diabetes plate","WHO healthy diet"],["Starch portion","Fiber source","Meal balance","Individual response"]),
("06 · Fats and heart health","Intermediate","Replacing some saturated-fat sources with unsaturated sources fits heart-healthy dietary guidance.",[
("Unsaturated fats","Common in nuts, seeds and many plant oils","Peanuts or suitable cooking oil","Choose appropriate amounts"),("Saturated fat","Present in ghee, butter and fatty meats","Large repeated ghee portions","Review pattern"),("Trans fat","Avoid industrial trans fats where present","Check packaged-food ingredients","Read labels"),("Sodium","High-salt foods add up across meals","Pickles, snacks and restaurant meals","Consider total day")],"Cook a familiar vegetable dish with a modest amount of oil and compare packaged side dishes for sodium.","Does one spoon of ghee determine heart health?","No. Evaluate repeated intake and the whole dietary pattern.","Compare one packaged snack with a minimally processed alternative.",["AHA dietary guidance","WHO healthy diet"],["Fat source","Portion","Sodium","Pattern"]),
("07 · Vitamins and minerals","Intermediate","Deficiencies require attention to food, context and sometimes testing or treatment.",[
("Iron","Food sources include beans and animal foods","Dal with vegetables","Investigate suspected deficiency"),("B12","Reliable sources include animal foods and fortified foods","Vegetarian diets may need planning","Discuss if intake is low"),("Vitamin D","Food alone may not resolve deficiency","Fortified food or supplement if prescribed","Avoid guesswork"),("Calcium","Needed for bone health","Dairy or suitable alternatives","Check total diet")],"A mostly vegetarian family can plan B12 sources while seeking clinician advice for a documented deficiency.","Can a generic multivitamin treat a measured deficiency?","Dose and cause matter; discuss the specific result with a clinician.","Write down dietary sources and questions for a care visit.",["NIH supplement library","ICMR-NIN Indian guidelines"],["Food source","Absorption","Lab context","Plan"]),
("08 · Hydration and drinks","Intermediate","Water needs differ with climate, activity, age and medical conditions.",[
("Water","Useful default beverage","Glass of water with a meal","Drink to needs"),("Sugary drinks","Can add sugar without much satiety","Sweet tea or bottled drink","Read added sugar"),("Coffee and tea","Caffeine timing can affect some people","Late evening coffee","Watch sleep or reflux"),("Restrictions","Heart or kidney disease may require a fluid plan","Prescribed fluid limit","Follow clinician guidance")],"Compare a sweetened drink label with water; don't invent a fixed daily water target for everyone.","Must everyone drink eight glasses?","No. Needs and restrictions vary.","Track drinks for one day and note added sugars.",["WHO healthy diet","FDA added-sugar label","NIDDK GERD diet"],["Water","Sugar","Timing","Individual need"]),
("09 · Food labels and portions","Intermediate","Compare products using serving size and per-serving nutrients.",[
("Serving size","Label values apply to stated amount","Two servings double label totals","Check quantity eaten"),("Added sugar","Label separates added sugar from total sugar","Sweet cereal comparison","Compare grams and %DV"),("Sodium","Sodium can be high in savory products","Pickle or instant soup","Compare per serving"),("Ingredient list","Shows ingredients in descending order by weight","Whole-grain claim vs ingredients","Inspect before buying")],"A snack has 8 g added sugar per serving but the package contains two servings; eating all gives 16 g.","Is 'natural' enough to judge a food?","No. Check the actual serving, ingredients and nutrient label.","Use the label calculator for your actual number of servings.",["FDA added-sugar label","AHA dietary guidance"],["Serving","Multiply","Compare","Decide"]),
("10 · Food safety and storage","Intermediate","Safe handling matters as much as nutrient content.",[
("Clean","Wash hands and surfaces","Prepare salad after handwashing","Reduce contamination"),("Separate","Keep raw animal foods from ready-to-eat foods","Different cutting boards","Avoid cross-contact"),("Cook","Reach safe internal temperatures","Use a food thermometer where relevant","Do not judge by color alone"),("Chill","Refrigerate perishables promptly","Cool leftover dal safely","Check time and temperature")],"Store cooked rice and dal promptly rather than leaving them at room temperature for many hours.","Does reheating always make unsafe leftovers safe?","No. Prevention through correct storage matters.","Review clean, separate, cook, chill in your kitchen.",["FoodSafety.gov four steps"],["Clean","Separate","Cook","Chill"]),
("12 · Cooking methods and culture","Intermediate","Healthy cooking can fit Indian and other food traditions without a special medical label.",[
("Boil/steam","Use water and heat with little added fat","Steamed idli and vegetables","Keep variety"),("Sauté/roast","Flavor with measured oil and spices","Vegetable sabzi","Watch added fat and salt"),("Ferment","Some traditional preparations are fermented","Idli batter","Handle safely"),("Spices","Flavor can reduce reliance on excess salt","Ginger, cumin or herbs","Do not claim a cure")],"Compare deep-fried snacks with roasted or steamed options while keeping the family cuisine familiar.","Is a spice a proven treatment for diabetes?","Culinary use is fine; it does not replace tested treatment.","Pick one cooking method to make a familiar meal easier to repeat.",["ICMR-NIN Indian guidelines","WHO healthy diet","NCCIH Ayurveda"],["Ingredients","Method","Portion","Routine"]),
("13 · Meal planning and budgets","Advanced","A reusable meal template can reduce effort while supporting variety.",[
("Plan first","Select a few repeatable meals","Dal + vegetable + grain rotation","Reduce decisions"),("Shop for pattern","Buy staple, produce and protein sources","Beans, greens, fruit, yogurt","Match the week"),("Prep safely","Batch cook and chill properly","Portioned dal in refrigerator","Avoid unsafe storage"),("Review fit","Adjust portions for household needs","Adult vs child plate","Keep it flexible")],"Build two lunches from shared dal and vegetables, changing grains and protein sides.","Must every day be perfectly balanced?","No. Review the pattern over time.","Use the meal-builder checklist for two family meals.",["ICMR-NIN Indian guidelines","FoodSafety.gov four steps"],["Plan","Shop","Prep","Adjust"]),
("14 · Nutrition for common conditions","Advanced","Condition-specific food choices should complement, not replace, medical care.",[
("Prediabetes/diabetes","Quality carbs, portions and plate balance matter","Rice with dal and non-starchy vegetables","Coordinate with treatment"),("High LDL","Whole dietary pattern and fat sources matter","More legumes, less frequent high-saturated-fat sides","Review with clinician"),("GERD","Individual triggers and meal timing can matter","Avoid late trigger meal if symptomatic","Track personal triggers"),("Kidney disease","Protein, potassium, fluid needs may change","Do not assume one 'renal diet'","Seek individualized advice")],"For a person with reflux and glucose concerns, change one meal variable at a time and discuss persistent symptoms or abnormal tests.","Can one online diet treat all conditions?","No. Medication, kidney function and other conditions alter the plan.","Prepare three focused nutrition questions for a clinician or dietitian.",["ADA diabetes plate","AHA dietary guidance","NIDDK GERD diet"],["Condition","Meal choice","Monitor","Care plan"]),
("15 · Supplements and claims","Advanced","Food-first habits and evidence checks help avoid costly or unsafe products.",[
("Claim vs evidence","A testimonial is not a controlled trial","'Detox' product advertisement","Check reliable source"),("Dose and interaction","More is not always safer","Herb plus prescription medicine","Ask pharmacist"),("Deficiency treatment","A diagnosed problem may require specific therapy","Low B12 or iron result","Discuss dose and cause"),("No replacement","Supplements do not substitute for needed care","Stop medicine for powder claim","Do not delay treatment")],"Compare a supplement marketing claim with its NIH fact sheet and discuss a real deficiency with a clinician.","Are all natural products safe?","No. Interactions, contamination and dose matter.","Look up one product in the NIH supplement library before buying.",["NIH supplement basics","NIH supplement library","NCCIH Ayurveda"],["Claim","Evidence","Risk","Decision"]),
]
